Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so it reads tab-separated input. Make one pull request that meets every acceptance line below.

Acceptance lines (each must hold at your PR head):
1. `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.
2. With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]` followed by a newline.
3. With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]` followed by a newline.
4. Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\n1,2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]` followed by a newline.
5. Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{"a\tb":"1\t2"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.
6. `npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.
7. README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.

Current state: bin/csv2json.js reads all of stdin and writes `JSON.stringify(parse(text))` plus a newline; it ignores process.argv. src/parse.js exports `parse(text, sep = ',')`, which already splits rows on /\r?\n/ and fields on `sep`. test/csv.test.js uses node:test and has a `cli` test that runs bin/csv2json.js via execFileSync. package.json has `"test": "node --test"`.

Steps:
1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether `--tsv` appears anywhere in `process.argv.slice(2)`. If it does, call `parse(text, '\t')`; otherwise call `parse(text)` exactly as now. Keep output formatting unchanged (compact JSON.stringify plus one trailing newline). Do not change src/parse.js unless you need to; it already supports the separator.
2. In test/csv.test.js (or a new file in test/), add tests that run `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` with execFileSync on tab-separated input and assert the exact stdout: at least `'a\tb\n1\t2\n'` -> `'[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]\n'`. Also add cases for the comma-in-field input (line 2) and the two-row input (line 3). Optionally add a test that input with tabs but no flag still gives `[{"a\\tb":"1\\t2"}]\n` (watch JS string escaping). Keep the existing tests.
3. In README.md, add a section showing `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and state that --tsv means the input is tab-separated (fields split on a single tab character). Keep the existing CSV example.
4. Check your work from the repository root:
   - `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo "exit=$?"`
   - `printf 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv`
   - `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv`
   - `printf 'a,b\n1,2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js`
   - `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js`
   - `npm test; echo "exit=$?"`
   Each output must match its acceptance line exactly, and npm test must exit 0.

Out of scope: quoting or escaping rules (RFC 4180), auto-detecting the separator, a general --sep/--delimiter option, --help, changing how unknown arguments are handled, changing JSON formatting or how the header row is used.